## 7. Style Reference

The locked **"Neural Signal Schematic"** house style — instrument-grade technical schematic with JetBrains-Mono annotation and single-alarm-color discipline, in the lineage of a high-end audio **patch-bay / modular-synth routing panel** or an aircraft signal-distribution diagram. Think *Teenage Engineering / Eurorack patch-panel precision* crossed with *Wait-But-Why diagrammatic clarity*, rendered in the dark clinical register already established across the Cognitive set. Reads as a real reconfigurable instrument you could operate, not an infographic.

## 8. What to Avoid

- **Do NOT draw a second gain/overdrive console (4.2's device) or a vertical level-up ladder (4.0's device).** This must read as *routing/reconfiguration* — pulling and patching cables — not amplitude or progression. If it looks like dials being turned up, it's wrong.
- **Do NOT light up more than one enhanced patch cable at once.** That silently endorses stacking and breaks the article's hardest rule ("one lever at a time"). Exactly one live patch.
- **Do NOT re-typeset the master swap table as an image.** The table must *become* the patch-bay; a screenshot-of-a-table is the lazy failure.
- **Avoid the smart-drug cliché kit:** glowing brain, lightbulb-head, gears-in-skull, floating pills/capsules, pharmacy flat-lay. These make the *drug* the hero; here the **swap discipline and the kept fuel** are the heroes.
- **Avoid red-flooding.** Nicotine-dependence, cyclical resets, huperzine-overload-risk and the modafinil-only-when-blown-sleep caution are all **amber**. The *only* red is the double-patched over-peak. (Note: the dropped-fuel racetam-headache from 4.0 stays an amber companion fault here — one red per poster.)
- **Avoid making it look aspirational / "graduation."** The article insists this is a narrow, rarely-needed, high-failure tier. The gate dimming most traces, and the "one lever, measured" sternness, must survive into the art.

